## Commands

| When | Run |
|---|---|
| During a design/implementation phase | Compile checks and targeted tests or probes for the scope just implemented. No formatting, linting or integrated gate after a slice or commit. |
| After all functional scope in the plan is implemented | `just fmt`, then `just check`: {% if with_rust or with_python %}format check, lints, {% endif %}tests{% if with_ast_grep %}, rules{% endif %}, ADR and agent lint |
| Then, as end-of-scope acceptance | `just test-all`: `check`{% if with_rust %} plus `just deps`{% endif %} |
| Format (mutating) | `just fmt`, once at the end of the scope (above) |
{% if with_rust -%}
| Dependency policy | `just deps`: exact workspace pins with a `docs/pins.md` row, one version per declared family (`[workspace.metadata.pins]` in `Cargo.toml`), and cargo-deny sources and licenses |
{% endif -%}
| Decisions | `just adr new <slug> --title "…"`, `just adr supersede ADR-NNNN <slug>`, `just adr index`, `just adr lint`, `just adr revisit` |
{% if with_docs_site -%}
| Documentation changes | `just docs-test` for publisher/resolver changes; `just docs-check` for publication. First run: `just bootstrap-docs`; preview: `just docs-serve`. No product gate solely for docs. |
{% endif -%}
| Tools present? | `just doctor` |
| Template changes | `copier update --defaults`, then review the diff like any other change (`.copier-answers.yml` is never edited by hand) |

{% if with_rust -%}
The Rust toolchain is pinned to {{ rust_toolchain }} in `rust-toolchain.toml`. The machine's
rustup default may be nightly, so don't pass `+nightly` or `+stable` to cargo in this workspace.
The repository's `.cargo/config.toml` sets 16 Cargo jobs, `sccache` as the compiler wrapper,
and incremental compilation off so the compiler cache can store workspace crates. Keep Clang
and mold as the linker route. Avoid changing `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`, rustflags, or worktrees during
ordinary development because those changes disrupt build reuse. For an isolated diagnosis,
override the config through Cargo's environment variables and report it.

{% endif -%}
Python is {{ python_version }} via `uv` (`.python-version`); run Python tools as `uv run …`.
{% if with_python %}The type checker is **pyrefly**, not pyright or mypy. {% endif %}The process
scripts are stdlib-only and run without a project environment.

## Writing code against libraries

- Library capability skills are pinned, offline indexes kept under `.claude/skills/` but
  gitignored and versioned outside this repo. They are repo-agnostic: a skill never cites this
  repository; the repository only picks libraries and pins. Use a matching skill **before**
  writing against an API rather than relying on memory, and list it in
  `.claude/skills/README.md`.
- For a library no skill covers, **Context7 is the first stop**, then primary sources. Check a
  skill's or document's version against `docs/pins.md` before transferring a claim. An empty
  search result is not evidence that a capability is absent.

## Testing rules

- **During implementation, only compile checks and targeted tests.** Validate each new piece of
  scope with a compile check and the focused tests or probes that exercise it. Integrated tests
  (`just check`, `just test-all`) wait until all functional scope in the plan is implemented;
  repeat them only for a failure or a subsequent material change.
- **No formatting or linting until all functional scope is implemented.** Don't run `just fmt`,
  formatters, linters or type checkers mid-plan: they add nothing during execution and rewrite
  code other than yours, which you then have to reassess. Run `just fmt` once at the end, then the
  integrated gates (which include the lint checks).
- **Validators are shared.** Invariant validators are library code, used by both tests and the
  production path. Don't write test-only copies.
{% if with_rust -%}
- Rust tests run under nextest in the release profile, so repeated runs reuse cached optimized
  code. Test data may be fresh, existing, or empty according to the test's purpose.
- **Contract snapshots** (insta), once adopted, run with `INSTA_UPDATE=no`. To accept a change,
  read the `.snap.new` diff first, then run `cargo insta accept`. Never run `cargo insta review`,
  which is interactive. A contract snapshot change is a migration, so say so in the commit.
{% endif -%}
{% if with_python -%}
- Python tests live under `tests/`; pytest runs with `--strict-markers --strict-config`.
{% endif %}
## Working agreements

- **Judgment over evidence ceremony.** Principles state properties; never add probes, tests or
  oracles merely as proof of diligence. Run a check when it resolves a real question.
- **No new alignment linting.** Don't add lints or checks for design alignment; update existing
  checks only when they break. Skills are welcome.
- **Probes go to the evidence folder**, `docs/design_review/evidence/<date>_<topic>/` with a
  README, not to a session scratchpad.
- **Detail over length.** Architecture pages have no line budget: the detail the design needs
  comes before brevity. History belongs in Git, not in the design.

## Reporting

- Report outcomes as `passed`, `failed`, `blocked` (name the missing prerequisite) or `not_run`,
  and give the command that produced each. A mocked provider is never `passed`. Never turn
  outcomes into a percentage.
- Design claims carry a principles §D label (`Proposed` … `Tested` … `Measured`). An unlabelled
  claim is a defect; `Proposed` is not.
- Date every "verified" claim.

## Decisions and reviews

- **Write an ADR** (the `adr` skill) when a change alters a §B decision, chooses between real
  alternatives, or would surprise a future session. Amend the owning architectural section in
  the same commit. To pivot, supersede the old ADR; accepted ADRs are immutable.
- **Keep a current working set** (ADR-0001). Update the owner whose meaning changed instead of
  appending history. When a plan, review, evidence folder or ADR loses its last current consumer,
  carry its surviving obligations forward and delete it; Git holds it
  ([historical recovery](docs/README.md#historical-recovery)).
- **Design reviews** use `design-review` and its declared profiles{% if with_ci_profile %}
  (`design-review-code-intelligence`){% endif %}, usually through the `design-reviewer` subagent. The binding's
  **Reviews in this repository** section owns cadence and tier/purpose selection (ADR-0002).
  Begin with responsibilities, contracts and expected changes; assess A1–A3 separately from
  correctness/fidelity gates. A bounded slice does not certify the enclosing architecture.
  Review depth follows impact and uncertainty.
- **Findings** retain stable source-review IDs. The active plan's findings table owns current
  disposition for scheduled work; unscheduled deferrals stay in the source review with a trigger
  (binding §4). Record the responsible component and closure evidence; link from follow-up
  reviews and STATUS. A decision being accepted does not establish implementation or verified
  closure.

## Git

- Work on `main` in the current working tree for ordinary edits, reviews and spikes. Use a
  separate worktree only when truly parallel agents must edit production code concurrently.
- Commit to `main` in small commits. Each message names the slice and any ADR, and states the
  test outcome. Never push unless asked.
- Never force-push or `reset --hard`.
- `.claude/skills/*` is gitignored except the process skills: `adr`, `design-review`,
  {% if with_ci_profile %}`design-review-code-intelligence`, {% endif %}`handoff`, `pin-check`.
- At the end of a session that changed what's true, run the `handoff` skill.
